<h1>should i take an unpaid internship or keep looking for a paid one</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">Take the unpaid internship only if it gives you real work you can prove. Otherwise, keep looking. The gap between "interned somewhere" and "can show what I built" matters more than the paycheck, and working for free without proof is just expensive networking with a fancy title.</p>

<div class="tldr">
<ul>
<li><strong>Unpaid internships favor the wealthy.</strong> Students who can afford to work without pay get the credential; everyone else gets shut out of the signal entirely.</li>
<li><strong>An internship cuts underemployment odds by 48.5%, but only when it leads to demonstrable skills.</strong> <a href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6777c52f82e5471a3732ea25/679a6fadfda4220bbac585d7_Talent-Disrupted-2.pdf">Strada Institute + Burning Glass Institute, "Talent Disrupted" (2024)</a></li>
<li><strong>63.1% of interns convert to full-time with the same employer.</strong> <a href="https://www.naceweb.org/">NACE (2024-25)</a> The catch: conversion depends on whether you did real work, not whether you were paid.</li>
<li><strong>Most unpaid internships violate labor law tests.</strong> The Department of Labor's primary beneficiary test is failed constantly, yet students take them anyway out of desperation for signal.</li>
<li><strong>Proof of work beats proof of presence.</strong> A scored project visible to recruiters replaces the internship's core function, without the access barrier.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<section>
<h2>why does unpaid even exist, and who actually benefits?</h2>

<p>Unpaid internships survive because employers and universities collude to call them "educational experiences." The employer gets free labor. The university gets placement statistics to advertise. The student gets a line on a resume and the hope of a reference.</p>

<p>The economics are brutal: you pay tuition for the credit, you pay rent to live in the expensive city where the internship is, and you earn nothing. The effective cost of a summer unpaid internship in New York or London runs thousands in foregone wages and living expenses. A luxury good masquerading as a career step.</p>

<p>The privilege filter is the point, not the bug. Industries that run on unpaid internships, fashion, media, politics, law, systematically exclude students who need to earn money during summers. The result is a workforce that looks like it does because the pipeline was designed to require parental subsidy.</p>

<p>zero is free for students because the access barrier is the broken part. You should not need to pay to prove you can work.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>when is an unpaid internship actually worth it?</h2>

<p>Three conditions, all mandatory: you do real work that builds a portfolio, you get meaningful mentorship from someone who will remember you, and the organization's name carries genuine signal with your target employers. Miss any one and you are paying to be furniture.</p>

<p>"Real work" means decisions with consequences. A marketing intern who actually ran a campaign, spent a budget, and can show results. A software intern who shipped code users touched. A research intern who co-authored or at least contributed analysis that went into a published piece. If your role was "shadowing," "assisting with," or "exposed to," you were not doing real work, you were observing, and observation is not a skill.</p>

<p>The name signal is weaker than you think. A famous company with a structured intern program is valuable, but a famous company where you fetched coffee is indistinguishable from a no-name company where you fetched coffee. Recruiters have learned to probe for substance, and "intern at [prestigious brand]" without specifics triggers skepticism, not admiration.</p>

<p>The mentorship condition is the most often violated. Will your manager spend 30 minutes weekly with you? Will they review your work and explain why it missed? Will they introduce you to people who hire? If the answer is vague promises, you are not getting mentorship, you are getting supervision, which is cheaper for them and worth less for you.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>what is the actual alternative to either kind of internship?</h2>

<p>The real question is not paid versus unpaid. It is whether you are building proof of skill or collecting credentials that proxy for it.</p>

<p>The old path requires you to find an institution willing to vouch for you, a university or an employer, and hope their brand carries weight. The new path is to build the work itself and let the work carry the weight. This is what zero does: company-shaped scenarios where you make real decisions, get scored against a professional bar, and produce recruiter-facing evidence of what you can do.</p>

<p>The degree is a mortgage on a depreciating asset: you borrow time and money for a credential whose value erodes as skills-based hiring expands. An unpaid internship is a smaller version of the same bad trade. You are still paying, just in a different currency, for someone else's endorsement rather than your own demonstrated capability.</p>

<p>Recruiters increasingly want to see the work, not the wrapper around it. <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">85% of employers now use skills-based hiring</a>, up from 81% the year before. The trend is toward proof, not pedigree. An unpaid internship that does not produce proof is a pedigree play in a market moving away from pedigree.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>how do i decide in the next 48 hours?</h2>

<p>If you have an unpaid offer in hand and a paid search ongoing, run this checklist:</p>

<p><strong>Demand specifics before accepting.</strong> What will you own? What will ship with your name on it? What will your manager say about you in a reference call, specifically? Vague answers mean vague value.</p>

<p><strong>Calculate the real cost.</strong> Foregone wages plus living expenses plus tuition for any credit. Compare that to what you could build in the same time with direct project work, freelance gigs, or zero scenarios that produce scored output.</p>

<p><strong>Check the legal status.</strong> In the US, unpaid internships must meet the Department of Labor's primary beneficiary test: the internship is similar to training given in an educational environment, the intern does not displace regular employees, the employer derives no immediate advantage. Most violate at least one prong. Knowing this gives you leverage to negotiate for pay or for better work conditions.</p>

<p><strong>Set a kill date.</strong> If you accept, decide now when you will quit if the work remains meaningless. Six weeks is usually enough to assess. Do not endure eight weeks of coffee runs because you are afraid to leave a "prestigious" line on your resume. The line is worthless if you have nothing to say about it.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>what if i cannot afford unpaid and cannot find paid?</h2>

<p>This is the honest bind, and the market's failure, not yours. The solution is not to accept exploitation gracefully. It is to build signal through channels that do not require institutional gatekeeping.</p>

<p>Direct projects are the strongest substitute. Build a tool, analyze a public dataset, write a case study, create a campaign for a company you admire without being asked. The work exists whether or not someone gave you permission to do it, and the difference is that permission-based work comes with a title while self-directed work requires you to explain its value. The explaining is harder but the proof is often stronger because you owned the entire stack.</p>

<p>Freelance or contract work, even at low rates, beats unpaid internship for signal because money changed hands. Someone valued your output enough to pay, which is a crude but real filter. The project may be smaller but the proof is cleaner.</p>

<p>zero scenarios are designed for this gap: structured enough that you know what to do, open enough that you own the decisions, scored so recruiters can compare your work to professional standards. The output is yours. The score is yours. The access is not gated by your ability to work for free.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
<h2>frequently asked questions</h2>

<div class="faq-item">
<h3>Do unpaid internships actually help me get hired?</h3>
<p>Only if they produce work you can demonstrate. The <a href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6777c52f82e5471a3732ea25/679a6fadfda4220bbac585d7_Talent-Disrupted-2.pdf">Strada Institute found that internships cut underemployment risk by 48.5%</a>, but this measures internships with real skill development, not title-only placements.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
<h3>Can I negotiate an unpaid internship into a paid one?</h3>
<p>Sometimes. If you have competing offers, even informal ones, use them. If the employer is deriving immediate advantage from your work, they are legally required to pay in most jurisdictions. Framing your ask around value delivered, not personal need, is more effective.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
<h3>Is a "stipend" or "transportation coverage" enough?</h3>
<p>No. A $500 monthly stipend against rent in a major city is still unpaid in function. Do not let partial coverage obscure the structural problem: you are working without fair compensation, and the organization knows it.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
<h3>What if the unpaid internship is at my dream company?</h3>
<p>Dream companies know they are dream companies and exploit that. The question is whether you will do dream work or dream-adjacent errands. Get explicit about projects and ownership before accepting. A famous name without substance is a weak signal in a skills-based market.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
<h3>How do I explain self-directed projects versus an internship on my resume?</h3>
<p>Lead with outcomes, not categories. "Built X that achieved Y" beats "intern at Z" every time when the achievement is specific and measurable. Recruiters scan for evidence of capability, not evidence of having been selected by an institution.</p>
</div>
</section>

